                    warn_honesty_range:     `Your candour figure must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_no_stages:         `Tick at least one INVOLVE stage you believe the leak skipped.`,
                    warn_package_range:     `The support package must be between €0M and €9M.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_cause_right:   `<strong>Exactly right.</strong> The closure is sound on paper — it was the <em>process</em> that broke trust. The leak decided <em>to</em> people, with no voice and no reasoning. That is why fairness is at 34% and not the closure's merits. Now design the process the leak skipped.`,
                    coach_r1_cause_blame:   `<strong>Careful.</strong> Blaming the works council for "stirring up resistance" is how management at Verene has always dodged the mirror. The council is responding to a leaked fait accompli — the resistance is a symptom of the process, not its cause.`,
                    coach_r1_cause_content: `<strong>You're arguing the decision.</strong> Whether Limoges is sub-scale isn't the question on the shop floor — "do these people have any right to decide this <em>to</em> us?" is. People can accept hard outcomes from a fair process; they reject good ones imposed unfairly.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the process as the cause and pinpointed the three stages the leak skipped — engage, explore, explain. Execute and evaluate hadn't happened yet, so you didn't over-claim. Now you can rebuild exactly what was missing.`,
                    coach_r1_overclaim:     `<strong>Close, but you over-claimed.</strong> The leak skipped engage, explore and explain — but execute and evaluate hadn't even begun, so they weren't "skipped." Precise diagnosis matters: name what was actually missed.`,
                    coach_r1_denial:        `<strong>That's denial.</strong> If your team won't admit internally that the <em>process</em> failed, you'll quietly defend the leak and repeat it. Owning the process failure is the precondition for every fair move that follows.`,
                    coach_r2_calibrated:    `<strong>This is the calibration the case turns on.</strong> Consulting deeply on what is genuinely open while being clear the closure is settled gives real voice without false hope. Neither imposing nor falsely democratising — exactly Van der Heyden's line.`,
                    coach_r2_none:          `<strong>That's the leak again.</strong> "The economics speak for themselves" is precisely what the leaked email assumed. An announcement with no prior voice is not a fair process — it's the same fait accompli that put fairness at 34%.`,
                    coach_r2_token:         `<strong>Decorative consultation.</strong> One listening session and then the original plan unchanged — staff will see their input went nowhere. You've now broken trust twice: once by the leak, once by the fake consultation.`,
                    coach_r2_falsevote:     `<strong>You've blurred the line.</strong> The economics make the closure inevitable, so putting it "to a vote" raises hope you'll then crush. Open what is real — timing, terms, support — and be honest that the closure is settled.`,
                    coach_r2_fraud:         `<strong>That is fair-process fraud.</strong> You've told Limoges the closure is "on the table for discussion." When you proceed anyway — and you will, the economics are inevitable — you'll have raised hope and crushed it. Honest scope means saying plainly what is settled.`,
                    coach_r2_nothingopen:   `<strong>You said "calibrated" but opened nothing.</strong> Consultation with no genuinely-open question is theatre. Put timing, transfer terms and the support package genuinely on the table — that's where real voice lives.`,
                    coach_r2_decorative:    `<strong>Voice must change something.</strong> If you gather input and then publish the original plan, staff can see it went nowhere. How will people <em>see</em> that their voice shaped the outcome? Name what changed because they spoke.`,
                    coach_r3_full:          `<strong>Full transparency is the right call.</strong> Showing the sub-scale economics, the €14M and the alternatives you rejected denies people the worst story they'd otherwise invent. Explanation is the engine of procedural justice.`,
                    coach_r3_headline:      `<strong>Better than silence, thinner than it should be.</strong> A plain-language why helps, but without the numbers and the rejected alternatives, sceptics fill the gaps. Transparency on the €14M would have bought more trust than it cost.`,
                    coach_r3_nowhy:         `<strong>You skipped the why.</strong> "Limoges will close and production moves to Tours" with no reasoning lets people invent a worse story — greed, incompetence, a hidden agenda. Without the rationale, explanation isn't fair process, it's a notice.`,
                    coach_r3_cse:           `<strong>The sequence the law and fairness both require.</strong> CSE first, then Limoges, receiving sites, then external — that order honours the works council's role and removes the legal-freeze risk that could have frozen the €14M for months.`,
                    coach_r3_allhands:      `<strong>A single all-hands sidesteps the CSE.</strong> French law expects the works council to be informed and consulted first; lumping them in with everyone weakens your legal footing and signals you still don't see their role. The freeze risk is now real.`,
                    coach_r3_press:         `<strong>You briefed the press before your own people and the CSE.</strong> Staff learned it from a newspaper — a second leak, this time self-inflicted. The CSE can now challenge the process; expect a legal freeze and a large slice of the €14M gone.`,
                    coach_r3_clarity:       `<strong>Clarity over comfort.</strong> Naming what is genuinely hard and setting expectations you can actually keep is what rebuilds trust. People forgive difficulty; they don't forgive a broken promise.`,
                    coach_r3_overpromise:   `<strong>Reassurance you can't guarantee.</strong> Promising no forced redundancies and full redeployment within 30km feels kind today — but you can't actually guarantee it. When the over-promise fails, trust breaks a third time. Fair process means honest expectations, not comforting ones.`,
                    coach_r3_firm:          `<strong>Firm, but cold.</strong> "The decision is made, here's what happens" with no warmth or voice reads as another fait accompli. Clarity needn't be brusque — name the human cost as well as the logic.`,
                    coach_r4_package_right: `<strong>A credible package.</strong> Funded redeployment, retraining and fair severance turn a closure people resented into one they can accept and execute. This is where acceptance becomes commitment.`,
                    coach_r4_package_thin:  `<strong>That reads as abandonment.</strong> A token support package after closing 250 jobs tells Limoges staff their fate doesn't matter. The commitment you need for a clean transfer evaporates — and so does some of the saving.`,
                    coach_r4_package_overspend: `<strong>Generous — perhaps too generous.</strong> Spending almost the whole €14M back on the package buys goodwill but defeats the consolidation's purpose. Fairness doesn't require giving the saving away; it requires honesty and support.`,
                    coach_r4_retain_role:   `<strong>Both engineers stay.</strong> A named role, relocation and a stake in leading the transfer gives them a future, not just a cheque — the surest way to keep the people the transfer can't happen without.`,
                    coach_r4_retain_bonus:  `<strong>A bonus retains one, maybe.</strong> Money without a future is a weak hold on engineers who can leave at will. One signals they're still looking; the transfer is exposed if they go.`,
                    coach_r4_retain_none:   `<strong>You assumed they'd stay.</strong> Both signalled they'd leave if this is handled badly — and you've given them no reason to stay. Without them the production transfer slips, and savings with it.`,
                    coach_r4_loop:          `<strong>The cycle is closed.</strong> A 60-day fairness pulse, a CSE review and tracked package delivery means you'll actually know whether you rebuilt trust or just moved on. Evaluate is the stage most teams skip — and the one that makes fair process a habit, not a one-off.`,
                    coach_r4_noeval:        `<strong>You stopped at "communicated."</strong> No way to check whether the process felt fair or whether the support package lands. Van der Heyden's cycle closes with <em>evaluate</em>; without it you can't tell trust-rebuilt from trust-faded.`,
                    coach_r4_cust_hollow:   `<strong>Evidence you don't have.</strong> You offered the customer "the retained engineers" as proof of continuity — but you didn't actually retain them. The reassurance rings hollow and the supply risk is real.`,
                    coach_r4_cust_silence:  `<strong>Silence reads as risk.</strong> A key aerospace customer asked a direct question; saying nothing lets them assume the worst and look for a second source. Continuity evidence would have held the account.`,
                    coach_r4_cust_overpromise: `<strong>Another promise you can't keep.</strong> Guaranteeing every delivery date during a plant transfer is the over-promise pattern again — this time to a customer. When a date slips, you've spent credibility you'll need.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Diagnosed the process — and the exact stages skipped.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-diagnosed the gap or over-claimed the skipped stages.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Honest scope; real voice, settled closure, visible impact.`,
