                    sum_r3_good:            `Transparent why, CSE-first sequence, clarity over comfort.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Skipped the why, wrong sequence, or over-promised.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Real package, engineers retained, evaluate loop closed.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Thin support, lost engineers, or no evaluate stage.`,

                    insight_fairprocess:    `Fair process: you rebuilt perceived fairness toward its 62% baseline, converted acceptance into committed execution, and kept process integrity high without fair-process fraud. The decision barely changed — but how you made it changed everything about whether people accept and execute it.`,
                    insight_fraud:          `Fair-process fraud: at some point you put the settled closure up for input, raising hope you then crushed. Van der Heyden is explicit — fake consultation is more corrosive than none, because it betrays trust twice. Honest scope was the whole game.`,
                    insight_lowintegrity:   `Process integrity stayed low: somewhere you imposed, falsely democratised, skipped the why, or over-promised. Procedural justice is built from honest scope, explanation and clarity — not from comfort or control.`,
                    insight_partial:        `A partial rollout: you avoided the worst errors but didn't fully honour the INVOLVE cycle. Some stages were thin, and the trust rebuild stalled short of the baseline.`,
                    insight_lowcommit:      `Commitment stayed low. Acceptance is not commitment — without genuine voice, a credible support package and a closed evaluate loop, people comply but don't carry the transfer.`,
                    insight_savingslost:    `You lost a large slice of the €14M — most likely to a CSE legal freeze, an anxiety-driven output slip, or losing the engineers the transfer depends on. Fair process protects the savings precisely by protecting trust.`,
                    insight_overpromise:    `You over-promised to calm anxiety — no forced redundancies, full redeployment, or a delivery guarantee you couldn't back. Clarity beats comfort: a promise you break is a future trust breach, and fair process means honest expectations.`,
                    insight_engineers:      `You didn't fully retain the two key engineers the transfer needs. People are the carriers of execution; losing them turns a clean consolidation into a delayed, costlier one.`,
                    insight_balance:        `Genuine fair process: fairness, commitment, integrity and savings moving together. You demonstrated Van der Heyden's central claim — a fair <em>process</em> wins acceptance, trust and execution even for a decision people dislike.`,

                    hint_fraud:             `You've put a settled decision up for input — that's fair-process fraud, the case's central trap. Re-scope honestly: name plainly what is open and what is settled.`,
                    hint_fairness:          `Perceived fairness is still near the post-leak floor. Stop announcing and start engaging — give genuine voice on what is open before you communicate anything.`,
                    hint_integrity:         `Process integrity is weak. Check for the classic errors: imposing, false openness, decorative consultation, skipping the why, or over-promising.`,
                    hint_savings:           `The €14M is bleeding away — most likely a legal freeze, an output slip, or losing the key engineers. Fair process is what protects the savings; protect the process.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong rollout — fairness near baseline, commitment and integrity high. Now close the loop: package, retention and a 60-day evaluate checkpoint.`,

